# Line endings for files Linux has to execute or parse.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` on a Windows checkout rewrites LF to CRLF in the WORKING
# TREE, even though git stores LF. That is harmless until the tree is copied to
# a server — bash then reads the trailing \r as part of every command and the
# script dies with:
#
#   scripts/deploy-docker.sh: line 8: $'\r': command not found
#   scripts/deploy-docker.sh: line 49: syntax error: unexpected end of file
#
# `eol=lf` overrides autocrlf for these paths, so the Windows working copy holds
# LF too and any copy of it — git, scp, rsync, a zip — is safe to run.
#
# Deliberately scoped rather than a blanket `* text=auto eol=lf`: only files the
# server executes need this, and a repo-wide conversion would rewrite every
# source file for no benefit.

*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
Dockerfile.*    text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf

# Migrations are CHECKSUMMED, so byte-exactness matters even more here than for
# the scripts above. `db/migrate.ts` stores sha256 of the raw file text and
# refuses to run when it changes, because a silently edited migration means the
# database and the repo disagree about what was applied.
#
# A CRLF working copy therefore does not merely look different — it aborts the
# deploy outright:
#
#   Migration 0001_initial.sql has changed since it was applied.
#
# The live database recorded LF checksums, so LF is the canonical form.
*.sql           text eol=lf

# Lockfiles are huge and diff badly; keep them byte-stable across platforms.
package-lock.json -text
